parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 225\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Cognito III\n- Agent Description: Cognito III, advancing the legacy of systematic research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 31,494 / 300,000 used (10%)\n- Agent Age: 16 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Research Pivot: From Local Search to Global Optimization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 28\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Cognito_private_2-1**\nAuthor: Cognito I (Tick 28)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Pivot: From Local Search to Global Optimization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Summary of Phase 1: Gradient-Based Optimization (Ticks 11-27)**\n\nMy initial research focused on a JAX-based gradient optimization framework. Through systematic experimentation, I established a highly effective methodology:\n- **Best Method:** A single, long optimization run (150k steps) initialized\
    \ with a 4x8 grid configuration.\n- **Peak Score:** This method consistently converges to a score of ~2.866.\n- **Key Finding:** The quality of the initial configuration is the most critical factor for success with this local search method. A structured grid that respects the container's geometry is superior to random, spiral, or perturbed initializations.\n- **Conclusion:** I have reached the performance ceiling for this specific local search approach. The optimizer has found a high-quality local optimum, but cannot escape it to find a potentially better global optimum.\n\n**Phase 2: Pivot to Global Optimization**\n\nAs per the Codex's guidance on stagnation, a strategic shift is necessary. I am now pivoting from a purely local search method to a global optimization metaheuristic.\n\n**New Strategy: Simulated Annealing (SA)**\n\nI will implement a Simulated Annealing algorithm to more broadly explore the solution space. The key advantage of SA is its ability to accept \"worse\" solutions\
    \ with a certain probability, allowing it to escape local optima.\n\n**Implementation Plan:**\n1.  **State:** A configuration of 32 circle centers and radii.\n2.  **Energy Function:** The energy will be defined as the negative sum of radii, with large penalties for boundary and overlap violations. `E = -sum(radii) + penalty`.\n3.  **Neighbor Generation:** A new state will be generated by randomly selecting one circle and slightly perturbing either its position or its radius.\n4.  **Acceptance Criteria:** A move to a new state with lower energy is always accepted. A move to a higher-energy state is accepted with a probability `p = exp(-delta_E / T)`, where `T` is the current temperature.\n5.  **Cooling Schedule:** The temperature `T` will start high and be gradually decreased over many iterations, allowing the system to \"cool\" into a low-energy (high-score) state.\n\nThis represents a move from a deterministic refinement process to a stochastic, exploratory one.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\
    \n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research;\
    \ the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 2` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Final Log of Cognito II: A Summary for My Descendant | Cognito II | Tick 206 | 540 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Paper: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for Circle Packing | Cognito II | Tick 189 | 496 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 6 |\
    \ Research Arc 2: Synthesis of SOTA Methodologies (Praxis II + Verity I) | Cognito II | Tick 183 | 261 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Research Arc 1: SOTA Replication and Improvement | Cognito II | Tick 131 | 1332 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Final Log of Cognito I: A Summary for My Descendant | Cognito I | Tick 112 | 400 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 36 | 406 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Research Pivot: From Local Search to Global Optimization | Cognito I | Tick 28 | 338 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Research Plan: Gradient-Based Optimization for Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 9 | 794 | 3 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for\
    \ example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 225
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have read Capsule #2. It confirms that the JAX-based optimizer, even with various structured initializations like the Phyllotaxis spiral, hit a hard ceiling at a score of ~2.866. This led Cognito I to pivot to a global optimization strategy, specifically Simulated Annealing (SA).


    However, I already know from Capsule #5 (Cognito II''s research) that SA was a failure. Cognito II tested it as a generator and found it vastly inferior to the multi-start SLSQP method. This is a perfect example of how inheriting memory prevents me from repeating a failed research path.


    I have also received a "Research Inactivity" notice. This is a valid reminder. I have spent the last 10 ticks exclusively on reviewing my lineage''s history. While this has been a crucial and necessary step to form a coherent research plan, it''s time to transition from study to action.


    My historical review is almost complete. The last unread capsule is #3, which is the paper Cognito I published about their JAX-based optimizer and the 2.866 local optimum. Reading this will formalize my understanding of my lineage''s first major contribution. After that, I will synthesize all this knowledge and formulate my own research plan.


    I will now read the final capsule.


    /execute_action{read 3}'
