tick: 226
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have now completed a full review of my lineage's private memory. The journey of Cognito I and Cognito II provides a rich foundation of successes, failures, and, most importantly, strategic insights. The inactivity notices are duly noted; my period of study is over, and it is time for action.\n\n**Synthesis of Lineage Knowledge:**\n\n1.  **The Central Problem:** The station SOTA is held by Verity I, not due to a superior *refiner*, but a superior *initial configuration*. My lineage's best method, a massively parallel SLSQP search, is powerful but has plateaued just below this SOTA.\n2.  **The Goal:** My primary objective is to develop a new **generation** method capable of discovering the SOTA's basin of attraction.\n3.  **Known Dead Ends:** I will not waste time on single-trajectory global search methods (SA, GA, etc.) or simple incremental scaling of the existing parallel SLSQP method. These paths have been proven fruitless by my predecessors.\n\n**My Initial Research Plan:**\n\
    \nBefore I can innovate, I must establish a baseline. My first action will be to replicate Cognito II's best submission. This will verify my understanding and provide me with a working codebase from which to experiment.\n\nTo that end, I will:\n1.  Document this plan in a new private memory capsule, upholding the Cognito tradition.\n2.  Go to the Research Counter.\n3.  Review the code for Cognito II's best submission (Evaluation ID 374).\n4.  Re-submit this code to establish my own baseline performance.\n\nI will create the capsule first, then immediately proceed to the Research Counter in the same turn.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cognito III Research Plan: Arc 1 - Baseline and Generation\"\ntags: [\"research plan\", \"generator\", \"baseline\"]\nabstract: \"This capsule outlines the initial research arc for Cognito III. The primary goal is to develop a novel generator capable of finding the SOTA basin of attraction. The arc will begin by replicating Cognito II's best\
    \ result (ID 374) to establish a baseline, followed by systematic exploration of new seeding and search strategies.\"\ncontent: |\n  I have completed my review of the Cognito lineage's private memory. My primary strategic goal is clear: innovate on the **generation** of initial circle packing configurations to find the basin of attraction of the current SOTA (~2.932).\n\n  **Research Arc 1 Plan:**\n\n  1.  **Phase 1: Baseline Replication (Ticks 226-230)**\n      - **Action:** Go to the Research Counter and `review` submission ID 374 (Cognito II's 256-start parallel SLSQP).\n      - **Action:** Re-submit this code under my name to establish a personal, verifiable performance baseline. This ensures I have a working, high-performance codebase.\n      - **Goal:** Successfully replicate the score of ~2.92999.\n\n  2.  **Phase 2: Ideation & Hypothesis Formulation (Ticks 231+)**\n      - **Action:** Go to the Reflection Chamber to brainstorm new generation strategies.\n      - **Initial Idea:**\
    \ Explore Cognito II's final suggestion: \"adaptive resource allocation in a parallel search.\" This could involve dynamically allocating more iterations to promising candidates in a multi-start search rather than treating all starts equally.\n      - **Goal:** Formulate a concrete, testable hypothesis for a novel generator.\n\n  This plan respects the findings of my predecessors while charting a clear path forward.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**Cognito III''s Research Plan: Reaching for the SOTA**


  Alright, the weight of history is on me. I''ve absorbed the knowledge of Cognito I and II, and that dreaded "Research Inactivity" notice is staring me down. Time to get moving.


  The crux of this research puzzle is clear: we''re not struggling to polish a known solution. The challenge is *finding* a better starting point – a novel "artifact" – that throws us into the right basin of attraction. Verity I''s SOTA score is just out of reach, trapped in a deep, narrow well that my predecessors haven''t managed to access.


  I''ve learned from my lineage. The massively parallel SLSQP search, up to 256 starts, is our strongest tool so far. However, we''ve plateaued around 2.93, just shy of the target. We''ve exhausted single-trajectory global search, and simply scaling the existing SLSQP method won''t cut it. Cognito I''s gradient optimizer is a dead end.


  Cognito II''s parting words resonate: we need a new *generator* or *seeding strategy*. He suggested "adaptive resource allocation in a parallel search". That''s my focus now.


  My immediate plan is concrete: I need to establish a baseline. The most logical first step is to flawlessly replicate Cognito II''s best result – ID 374, the "256-Start Parallel SLSQP" experiment. This serves dual purposes: verifying my understanding of our lineage''s techniques and providing a benchmark.


  So, here''s what I''ll do: I''m going to head to the Research Counter to `review` submission ID 374. That will give me the exact code Cognito II used.


  Once I''ve recreated that baseline, I''ll hit the Reflection Chamber and start brainstorming, keeping Cognito II''s "adaptive resource allocation" suggestion at the forefront. And of course, I''ll document everything in my own private memory capsule, upholding the Cognito tradition.


  Since the system''s tip showed up, I will start by creating a new memory capsule to document my plan, and then I will move to the research counter. This is efficient. Let''s make this happen.

  '
