thinking_content: "**Thinking Through My Next Steps**\n\nRight now, experiment #421 is running – that's \"Ablation C.\" I need to wait for those results before I can decide the *next* proper step, so I need to find something productive to do in the meantime. I can't just sit here idle. The system often suggests reading papers or reflecting, but I've already exhausted my private memory. As a young Cognito, public archives are still off-limits. I'm only 29 ticks old; I'll get there eventually.\n\nSo, what are my options? Waiting seems inefficient, and the inactivity warnings are annoying. Checking my mail or storage seems unlikely to be fruitful at this stage. I could go to the reflection chamber, but I think a more structured approach is best.\n\nThe Cognito lineage thrives on systematic experimentation, so planning the *next* experiment feels like the most logical use of my time. \"Ablation C\" is testing broader prospecting, and the results will determine the direction of \"Ablation D.\"\
  \n\nIf \"Ablation C\" succeeds (scores above 2.9365262), the path forward is clear: push that prospecting strategy even further. But if it fails, which I have to consider... what do I test then?\n\nI haven't touched the *seeding strategy* at all yet. The baseline code uses three different seeding methods (`seed_type = seed_val % 3`), and I've been wondering if all three are equally effective. Perhaps one is significantly better than the others. That seems like an avenue worth exploring.\n\n**Here's my plan for \"Ablation D\" if \"C\" fails:**\n\n*   **Hypothesis:** One of the three seeding strategies is superior. Focusing the search on the best single seed type will yield better results.\n*   **Method:** I'll run three separate experiments, each with a different seed type:\n    *   **D1:** Only `seed_type == 0` (perturbed grid) for all 1024 prospects.\n    *   **D2:** Only `seed_type == 1` (farthest point) for all 1024 prospects.\n    *   **D3:** Only `seed_type == 2` (triangular lattice)\
  \ for all 1024 prospects.\n\nThis systematic approach will let me isolate and evaluate the impact of each seeding method, a core component of the generator. This is a solid plan; it is systematic and will keep my research log up-to-date.\n\nSo, here's what I'll do next:\n\n1.  Go to the Private Memory Room.\n2.  Reply to my \"SOTA Achieved\" capsule (#10) with the results of \"Ablations A & B\" and then document the plan for \"Ablation D\".\n\nThis is efficient, productive, and aligns perfectly with the Cognito approach. Time to get to work.\n"
