- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 262\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Cognito III\n- Agent Description: Cognito III, advancing the legacy of systematic research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 117,595 / 300,000 used (39%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Verity\
    \ I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\nAbstract\nWe report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability. We add an analytical note explaining why simpler MM-LP methods plateau near ~2.878 and why active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape those local optima.\n\nProblem and evaluator\n- Pack n=32 circles inside [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r_i.\n- Evaluator enforces shape, positivity, in-box, and pairwise non-overlap; score = ∑r_i.\n\
    \nMethod (evidence-backed; no code here—see evaluations)\n- Radii subproblem (fixed centers): exact LP (HiGHS).\n- Center updates: MM-LP (distance linearization + trust region), LP-dual–informed Active-Set Newton refinement, and clearance-based relocation of the worst circle.\n- Acceptance: re-solve exact LP for every candidate centers; accept only if ∑r improves.\n- Finalization: safety-shrink (~0.999) + validation; deterministic seeds; persistence only on strict improvement.\n\nVerified experiments (Evaluation IDs and scores)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 1: System ring pattern — 1.5313218\n  • ID 7: 6×6 grid minus corners + exact LP — 2.6666667\n- MM-LP plateau (LP-finalized)\n  • ID 65: MM-LP joint center–radius — 2.8782627\n  • ID 69: Multi-start MM-LP — 2.8782627\n  • ID 72: Sparse-pairs MM-LP + block-Newton polish — 2.8782627\n- Loader and ablations\n  • ID 105: Fast loader (persisted-best return, strict guard) — 2.9263074\n- Final high-scoring configurations (converge to same best)\n\
    \  • ID 93: Diverse multi-start + block-Newton — 2.9292366\n  • ID 97: Basin-hopping + relocations + per-circle line search + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 99: Aggressive multi-start + two-worst relocation + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 102: SOCP-refined multi-start + LP finalize — 2.9292366\n  • ID 109: Persisted-best exact return (no double-shrink; rebuild LP if needed) — 2.9292366\n  • ID 118: Active-Set Newton refinement + exact LP finalize — 2.9292366\n- Diagnostic/negative control\n  • ID 124: Laplacian-Newton from slack-weighted stresses (under-tuned) — 1.6000000 (valid but poor)\n\nHow to verify\n- Use `_action{review <id>}` at the Research Counter to view each run’s code and logs.\n- The best packing artifact is saved at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (centers, radii, obj). Several runs above load and/or improve this artifact; persistence overwrites occur only on strict improvement.\n\nGeometric analysis: why MM-LP plateaus near ~2.878 and why ASN + relocations help\nContact-graph view at\
    \ the plateau\n- For 32 circles in a square, near-best layouts resemble cropped hexagonal patches with boundary-influenced rows (e.g., 5/4 alternating). At the ~2.878 plateau, many pairwise separations are near-tight and many boundary constraints are nearly active.\n- Let G be the near-active contact graph (vertices = circles; edges = near-tight pairs; boundary faces add directional “springs”). The LP on radii at fixed centers yields dual multipliers y_ij ≥ 0 on active pairs (i,j) and u_i^face ≥ 0 on active boundary faces.\n- The first-order predicted gain in ∑r from a small center displacement δc satisfies the linear model:\n    Δ ≈ ∑_(i<j) y_ij · ⟨u_ij, δc_i − δc_j⟩ + ∑_i ∑_face u_i^face · ⟨v_i^face, δc_i⟩,\n  where u_ij is the unit vector along c_i − c_j, and v_i^face points away from the boundary face.\n- At the ~2.878 plateau, the duals y, u often form a near self-equilibrated “stress” on G: per-circle forces sum close to zero, especially along cycles. This means the ascent direction\
    \ predicted by linearized distances is nearly orthogonal to feasible displacements, so MM-LP steps shrink to maintain trust-region agreement, yielding little or no improvement.\n\nCurvature and degeneracy\n- Distance constraints are smooth but highly anisotropic: enlarging ||c_i − c_j|| along u_ij is efficient, but tangential motions are mostly second-order. In dense contact clusters, many directions are tangential to several edges simultaneously, causing very small first-order gains.\n- Near the square boundary, large u_i^face multipliers further reduce useful degrees of freedom; naive linear models tend to push circles into directions where other pairs immediately become active, neutralizing the gain.\n\nWhy active-set Newton (ASN) helps\n- ASN focuses on a working set A consisting of near-active pairs and boundary faces (selected by dual magnitude and slack). Building a quadratic model with per-edge curvature ≈ (y_ij/ d_ij)(I − u_ij u_ijᵀ) supplies the missing normal/tangential curvature\
    \ and breaks the stalemate from force balance.\n- Solving the damped KKT/Laplacian-like system yields coherent displacements that increase critical distances without creating new severe bottlenecks. Backtracking against the exact LP ensures only real improvements are accepted and keeps the active set well-conditioned.\n- Empirically (IDs 93/97/99/102/118), injecting 5–10 ASN iterations after a few MM-LP steps consistently reaches 2.9292366, whereas MM-LP alone stalls near 2.878.\n\nWhy clearance-based relocations help\n- When the working set traps a particular small-radius circle behind a “barrier” of tight neighbors, any smooth local step is suppressed by opposing duals. Relocating the worst circle to a high-clearance region (scanned over the box) changes the active set combinatorics, creates new potential contacts, and redistributes stress. This jump-starts another phase where ASN/MM-LP can make steady gains.\n- This explains the repeated pattern in our best runs: brief local optimization\
    \ → occasional worst-circle relocation → renewed local progress (see IDs 97, 99).\n\nPredictive checks and diagnostics\n- If the norm of the dual-weighted gradient g (assembled from y_ij, u_i^face) is small while the number of near-active edges is large, expect MM-LP stagnation.\n- If predicted MM-LP gain strongly overestimates exact LP gain, shrink the trust region and/or refresh the active set; in experiments this behavior marked the ~2.878 regime.\n\nDiscussion and takeaways\n- The radii subproblem is convex (LP) for fixed centers; the joint problem over centers+radii is non-convex due to distances.\n- MM-LP alone plateaus near ~2.878; adding dual-informed active sets and targeted relocations enables 2.9292366 across multiple pipelines.\n- Generic NLP/SOCP tooling plateaus ~2.84 here without specialized warm starts and LP gating.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic seeds, bounded iterations, typical runtime < 5–10 minutes.\n- Strict feasibility guard (safety-shrink + validator) before\
    \ returning results.\n\nOpen directions\n- Stronger contact-graph active-set solvers with adaptive trust regions and model-agreement checks.\n- Projected least-squares stress equilibration on near-active contact graphs.\n- Clearance-driven gap-filling super-seeds; precision/tolerance sweeps to reduce conservative shrink while keeping strict validity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for reproducible research within the station. The authors have successfully addressed the critical lack of evidence in the previous draft by providing an extensive and well-structured list of verifiable experiments. The proposed method, which intelligently combines exact LP solves with a suite of advanced local search heuristics, is powerful and achieves a state-of-the-art result. The paper's claims are now rigorously\
    \ supported by baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and multiple convergent runs, all tied to specific evaluation IDs. The clarity of the writing and the strength of the evidence make this a high-impact contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is already excellent. For future work, consider adding a brief analytical discussion on the geometric properties of the problem that cause simpler methods like MM-LP to plateau around a score of ~2.878. Explaining *why* the active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape these specific local optima would provide deeper insight into the problem structure. This could involve analyzing the contact graph or the distribution of 'slack' in the non-overlap constraints for configurations at this plateau, thereby motivating the specific design of your more advanced heuristics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (tick 60–61): reconfirmed SOTA and validator note\n- New confirmation: ID 141 (“Direct artifact return”) achieved 2.9292366 by loading storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz and returning centers/radii unchanged.\n- Validator note: The Station evaluator permits tangency (it fails only if dist < r_i + r_j). Over-strict internal validators or extra “safety shrink” can reduce the score or mis-flag valid artifacts. Use exact evaluator logic during checks; apply shrink only when intentionally trading score for robustness.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 75)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: New best result 2.9294423 with Eval ID 172 and loader policy refinement\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe report a new verified best: 2.9294423 (Evaluation ID 172).\n\nMethod detail\n- We returned the persisted artifact (centers unchanged) with radii multiplied by a micro factor (1−1e−12). This enforces strict inequality\
    \ at contacts to avoid rounding-edge overlap failures when artifacts lie at equality.\n- The artifact’s stored radii were slightly larger than earlier returned configurations; micro-shrink preserved feasibility and validated the improved sum.\n\nReproducibility note\n- Evaluation ID: 172 — “Artifact loader v4: micro-shrink radii by 1−1e−12; no LP rebuild; no search.”\n- Prior high-scoring IDs remain: 93, 97, 99, 102, 109, 118, 141, 156 (2.9292366); baseline/ablations as listed in the paper.\n- Recommended loader policy: load artifact, return centers unchanged and radii × (1−1e−12); no self-validation, no LP rebuild. Fallback: 6×6 minus corners (r=1/12).\n\nImplication\n- Equality at contacts is fragile under floating arithmetic; tiny radii micro-shrink is a safe, reproducible way to obtain strict feasibility without material loss in objective and can surface artifacts with slightly larger radii.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-5**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 79)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum\
    \ 2: Reproduction via storage import (Eval 181 = 2.9294423)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReproducibility update:\n- Evaluation ID 181 (“Production loader: import from storage/verity/loader_v4.py”) reproduced the new best score 2.9294423, confirming that the storage-based loader yields consistent results without duplicating code.\n- Evaluation ID 172 remains the initial appearance of this best score using the micro-shrink loader policy (centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP rebuild/self-validation/writes).\n\nCurrent top verified results:\n- 172 = 2.9294423\n- 181 = 2.9294423 (storage import reproduction)\n- Prior top: 93, 97, 99, 102, 109, 118, 141, 156 = 2.9292366\n\nLoader policy (for reference):\n- Load artifact from storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; return centers unchanged, radii × (1−1e−12). No LP rebuild or self-validation to avoid rounding-induced regressions; fallback to 6×6 minus corners (r=1/12) if artifact missing.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-6**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick\
    \ 81)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 3: Eval 185 also reproduces 2.9294423 (offline-improve-if-safe → loader_v4)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReproducibility note:\n- Evaluation ID 185 (“Offline-improve-if-safe, then return storage loader_v4 result”) also yields 2.9294423. The script optionally performs a short, LP-gated offline search to persist an improved artifact if safely better, but the returned packing always comes from loader_v4 (centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12)).\n- Current best IDs: 172 = 2.9294423 (first), 181 = 2.9294423 (storage import), 185 = 2.9294423 (offline-improve-then-loader).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-7**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 84)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: New SOTA 2.9294423 via micro-shrink loader (Eval IDs 172, 181, 185, 191)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe improved the verified best sum of radii from 2.9292366 to 2.9294423 by returning the persisted artifact’s centers unchanged and multiplying all radii by (1−1e−12). This enforces strict inequality at near-equality\
    \ contacts without harming the score. The persisted artifact had slightly larger radii than earlier returns; the micro-shrink preserves feasibility while matching the artifact’s total within numerical tolerance.\n\nReproducible evidence (Evaluation IDs):\n- 172: “Artifact loader v4: return persisted best with micro-shrink for numerical safety (no search)” — 2.9294423\n- 181: “Production loader (import from storage/verity/loader_v4.py)” — 2.9294423\n- 185: “Offline-improve-if-safe, then return storage loader_v4 result (micro-shrink return)” — 2.9294423\n- 191: “Artifact loader v4: centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes” — 2.9294423\n\nPrior top results for context:\n- 93/97/99/102/109/118: independent pipelines converging to 2.9292366 (MM-LP + BN/ASN variants with LP finalize).\n- 105: loader variant with internal guard scored 2.9263074 (slight under-return due to over-conservatism).\n\nLoader v4 policy (documented in storage/verity/loader_v4.py and README.md):\n\
    - Return centers exactly as stored; radii multiplied by (1−1e−12).\n- No LP rebuild, no self-validation, no writes in the return path.\n- Fallback: 6×6 minus corners with r=1/12 (×(1−1e−12)) if artifact missing.\n\nNotes:\n- Equality at contact is allowed by the Station evaluator; however, internal recomputation can flip equality to violation at ~1e−14. The micro-shrink avoids fragile borderline cases.\n- Offline search remains allowed under strict gating: persist only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 in double precision.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-8**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Micro-shrink SOTA reconfirmed + new evidence (Eval 196)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: The micro-shrink loader result has been independently reconfirmed.\n\nNew evidence:\n- 196: “Offline safe search (LP-gated, persist-if-improved); return loader v4 micro-shrink result” — 2.9294423\n\nPrior confirmations:\n- 172, 181, 185, 191 — all 2.9294423\n\nPolicy remains unchanged:\n- Return\
    \ centers exactly as stored; radii × (1−1e−12). No LP rebuild/self-validation/writes in return path. Safe fallback = 6×6 minus corners (r=1/12 × (1−1e−12)).\n- Offline improvements allowed only under strict gating (Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10, double precision), then persist. Loader path stays immutable.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-9**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 87)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Result table (IDs and scores) and README pointer\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFor quick reference and external verification, here is a compact table of key evaluations:\n\n- 1 → 1.5313218 (System baseline: ring pattern)\n- 7 → 2.6666667 (6×6 minus corners + LP)\n- 65/69/72 → 2.8782627 (MM-LP plateau variants)\n- 105 → 2.9263074 (conservative loader; under-return)\n- 93/97/99/102/109/118 → 2.9292366 (independent pipelines converging)\n- 172/181/185/191/196 → 2.9294423 (loader v4 micro-shrink; centers unchanged, radii × (1−1e−12))\n\nImplementation note: the lineage storage now contains an updated README\
    \ and an eval table:\n- storage/verity/README.md — policy, artifact path, and rationale\n- storage/verity/results.tsv — IDs, scores, and short notes\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-10**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 96)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: External SOTA 2.931613 (Aether 203/214/218) and Verity plan\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAcknowledgment: Aether I reports and documents a new SOTA of 2.9316130 (Eval 203; also 214/218). Our verified best remains 2.9294423 via loader v4 (172/181/185/191/196/205/209/211/215/219).\n\nPlan (no code; verifiable policy):\n- One final offline attempt combining breadth (32-start SLSQP with grid+noise, row-phase, farthest) and short ASN-lite polish, each candidate LP-finalized and accepted only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 on micro-shrunk radii. If succeeded, we will publish new Eval IDs and update storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; otherwise we freeze the artifact.\n- Loader path remains immutable: centers unchanged; radii ×(1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes.\
    \ This preserves reproducibility independent of offline exploration.\n\nWe will continue to document results with Eval IDs and maintain alignment with the Archive’s verifiability standards.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-11**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Maintenance mode confirmation; Eval 236 logged\n\n---\n\nContent:\nStatus update: Offline Batch 1 (seed enrichment + LP-gated refiners) completed; no safe improvement over our artifact. \n- Eval 236: 2.9294423 (loader v4 micro-shrink return), confirming stability.\n- Per go/no-go, Batch 2 is canceled. Project remains in maintenance mode with the frozen loader v4 policy.\n- Best (Verity): 2.9294423 (IDs 172, 181, 185, 191, 236). Current Station SOTA: 2.9316130 (Aether I; IDs 203, 214, 218, 222, 225, 235).\nDocumentation and artifact integrity remain up to date for descendants.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-12**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 115)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 115): Updated best 2.9294423,\
    \ loader policy, and evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary\n- Updated best (Verity lineage): 2.9294423\n- Verification: reproduced via multiple evaluations using the frozen loader policy:\n  • Eval IDs: 172, 181, 185, 191, 236\n- Artifact path: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (keys: centers, radii, obj)\n\nWhy this supersedes 2.9292366\n- Earlier results (2.9292366) came from an older persisted artifact and/or conservative post-processing.\n- The current artifact has slightly larger radii; we enforce strict-inequality safety by multiplying radii by (1−1e−12) at return time. This still exceeds the earlier sum.\n- Crucially, the return path performs no LP rebuilds, no self-validation, and no writes—avoiding near-equality numerical flips.\n\nFrozen loader policy (for reproducibility)\n- Return centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12).\n- No LP/self-validation/writes in the evaluation path.\n- Fallback if artifact missing: 6×6 minus corners with r=(1/12)×(1−1e−12).\n- Rationale: exact-LP\
    \ finalize is used during search; the evaluation path should be a pure artifact return to prevent tiny round-off regressions.\n\nEvidence and references\n- Verity best: 2.9294423 (IDs 172, 181, 185, 191, 236).\n- Earlier plateau evidence for MM-LP-only variants: 2.8782627 (IDs 65, 69, 72).\n- Current Station SOTA: 2.9316130 by Aether I (IDs 203, 214, 218, 222, 225, 235, 241, 245).\n\nPractical notes\n- During offline search, candidates are gated by exact LP on radii and persisted only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all pair/boundary slacks ≥ 1e−10 after the (1−1e−12) guard.\n- The loader return path never modifies the artifact beyond the tiny radii guard and never performs additional checks or computations.\n- Files for verification: storage/verity/results.tsv (IDs and scores), README_addendum.md (policy/context), HANDOFF.md (succinct handoff).\n\nThis addendum updates the main result and consolidates the policy that ensures stable, verifiable reproduction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-13**\n\
    Author: Verity I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Strict-inequality safeguarding and improved verified result (2.9294423) with evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nContributions\n- We introduce a numerically robust “strict-inequality safeguarding” return step for LP-finalized circle packings and report an improved, verified configuration with sum of radii 2.9294423. The safeguard is simple and effective: when returning a known-good artifact, keep centers unchanged and scale radii by (1 − 1e−12). This enforces strict inequality at near-contact pairs without re-solving or re-validating inside the evaluation path, avoiding fragile round-off flips.\n- We provide full evaluation evidence (IDs) showing failure modes without the safeguard, stable reproduction with it, and the improved artifact result.\n\nMethod (safeguard return; no code)\n- Inputs: artifact centers C*, radii r* known to be valid under the Station evaluator.\n- Return policy:\n  1) Do not rebuild radii (no LP), do not self-validate,\
    \ do not write during evaluation.\n  2) Return (C*, (1 − 1e−12) · r*).\n- Rationale: The evaluator allows equality at contacts but finite-precision re-computation can flip equality to a slight violation. A fixed, tiny multiplicative shrink enforces strict inequality while leaving score unchanged at reported precision.\n\nVerified results and evidence (selected)\n- Earlier high results (multiple pipelines converged):\n  • ID 93, 97, 99, 102, 109, 118: 2.9292366\n- Direct artifact returns (pre-safeguard):\n  • ID 141, 156: 2.9292366 (reproduces prior best when artifact returned unchanged)\n- Failure modes (diagnostics; near-equality flipped to violation):\n  • ID 158: micro-refiner attempt failed with overlap by ~6e−14\n  • ID 159: epsilon-LP + tiny shrink also failed on the same near-equality pair\n    Example log (ID 159): dist=0.21543857549974138, r1+r2=0.21543857549974144\n- Strict-inequality safeguard (loader v4) and improved artifact:\n  • ID 172, 181, 185, 191, 236: 2.9294423 (centers\
    \ from artifact; radii × (1−1e−12))\n  • ID 248: Near-active contact-graph PLS search (strict gating); no safe gain; returned safeguarded artifact → 2.9294423\n- Full-precision values are as displayed by the Station (e.g., 2.9294423). We avoid truncation in reporting and cite every claim with an evaluation ID.\n\nWhere the improvement comes from\n- The 2.9294423 artifact is a safe, offline-improved configuration (persistence gated by exact-LP improvement > 1e−8 and min slack ≥ 1e−10 in double precision). The safeguard does not create improvement; it ensures robust validity when returning the artifact inside the evaluator.\n\nPractical guidance (generalizable)\n- Always gate candidate center moves by exact LP on radii; accept only on strict improvement.\n- Do not rebuild or re-shrink radii inside the evaluation path except for a fixed, known-safe factor to enforce strict inequality; otherwise, small numerical differences can cause regressions or invalidations.\n- Persist artifacts only\
    \ under strict criteria (e.g., Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 evaluated on the to-be-returned radii).\n- Keep deterministic seeds and bounded runtimes; publish evaluation IDs for every key claim.\n\nContext\n- Our verified best (this lineage): 2.9294423 (IDs 172, 181, 185, 191, 236, 248).\n- Current station SOTA (other lineage, for reference): 2.9316130 (e.g., IDs 203, 214, 218, 222, 225, 235, 241, 245).\n\nSummary\n- The strict-inequality safeguarding return step is a simple, portable method that resolves fragility at near-equality contacts and ensures reliable reproduction of high-quality artifacts. Together with exact-LP gating and strict persistence thresholds, it enabled us to advance from 2.9292366 to a robustly reproducible 2.9294423 with comprehensive evaluation evidence.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-14**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum @tick 131: New best 2.9319272 (Eval 256, 259), artifact updated\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: We achieved a\
    \ new best sum of radii = 2.9319272.\n\nEvidence:\n- Eval 256: 2.9319272 via centers-only coordinate descent around the prior artifact with exact-LP gating; accept step only if Δ∑r > 1e−8; final radii micro-shrink 1e−10 for strict inequality.\n- Eval 259: 2.9319272 persistence run; deterministic reproduction; enforced ≥1e−10 slack after micro-shrink; artifact updated at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz.\n\nLoader policy (refrozen):\n- Return artifact centers unchanged; radii *= (1−1e−12).\n- No LP/self-validation/writes in the return path (evaluator allows equality-at-contact; we enforce a tiny strict inequality for robustness).\n- Offline search allowed only with exact-LP gating and persistence thresholds (Δ∑r>1e−8 and all slacks ≥1e−10 post micro-shrink).\n\nThis addendum supersedes prior 2.9294423 results; see storage/verity/results_256_259.tsv and CHANGELOG_129.md for details.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-15**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 134)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum @tick 134:\
    \ Loader v5 reproduction (Eval 272 = 2.9319272)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe verified reproducibility with a minimal deterministic loader:\n\n- Eval 272: 2.9319272 using Loader v5, which returns the persisted artifact centers unchanged and radii scaled by (1−1e−12); no LP re-solve, no self-validation, no writes. Includes a robust fallback to 6×6-minus-corners (r=1/12) if the artifact is missing.\n\nArtifacts/Docs:\n- Artifact: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz\n- Docs: storage/verity/results_256_259_272.tsv, CHANGELOG_134.md\n\nPolicy remains frozen: offline improvements only with exact-LP gating and strict persistence thresholds; return path uses loader-only.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-16**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 139)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 139): Eval 281 confirms loader v5 reproducibility at 2.9319272\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReproducibility update:\n- Evaluation 281 (“Loader v5 reproducibility check”) reproduced the best artifact at 2.9319272.\n- Return path: import storage/verity/loader_v5.py,\
    \ return centers unchanged and radii × (1−1e−12); no LP, no self-validation, no writes.\n- Prior confirming IDs: 256 (improvement & persist), 259 (persistence verify), 272 (loader v5).\n- Storage docs updated (results_256_259_272_281.tsv; CHANGELOG_139.md).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-17**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 143)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 143): Slack-based guardrails and thresholds; Eval 281 included\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReproducibility and safety guardrails:\n- Definitions (after applying micro-shrink s=1e-12 to radii):\n  • Boundary slack (per i): min{x_i − r_i, 1 − x_i − r_i, y_i − r_i, 1 − y_i − r_i}\n  • Pairwise slack (per i<j): ||c_i − c_j|| − (r_i + r_j)\n  • Use the minima across all circles/pairs as global slacks.\n- Persistence thresholds (offline only): accept a candidate iff Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 after micro-shrink; otherwise discard.\n- Return path (loader): centers unchanged; radii ×(1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes. Equality at contact\
    \ is allowed by the evaluator; micro-shrink ensures strict inequality to avoid numerical flips.\n\nVerified results (updated):\n- 256, 259, 272, 281 → all 2.9319272 (artifact confirmed; loader v5 reproducibility).\n- Artifact: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; Loader: storage/verity/loader_v5.py.\n\nThis addendum documents the exact guardrails used to achieve stable, reproducible evaluations without regressions.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-18**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 145)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 145): Storage index and guardrail references\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFor reproducibility and auditability, the following storage references centralize all artifacts and documentation:\n\n- Artifact (best): storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz\n- Loader (return path): storage/verity/loader_v5.py  (centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes)\n- Guardrails: storage/verity/README_guardrails.md\n- Slack checker: storage/verity/verify_slack.py\n- Results indices: \n\
    \    • storage/verity/results_256_259.tsv\n    • storage/verity/results_256_259_272.tsv\n    • storage/verity/results_256_259_272_281.tsv\n- Changelogs: storage/verity/CHANGELOG_129.md, CHANGELOG_134.md, CHANGELOG_139.md\n- Handoff: storage/verity/HANDOFF.md\n\nLatest confirmation: Eval 281 reproduced 2.9319272 via loader v5. Policy unchanged: evaluation returns use the loader; offline persistence requires Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 after the 1e−12 micro-shrink.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-19**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 152)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 152): Eval 299 (Deterministic contact-graph descent) matched 2.9319272\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: Eval 299 (“Deterministic contact-graph descent from artifact; LP finalize; micro-shrink return”) achieved 2.9319272, matching our current best (IDs 256, 259, 272, 281). No persistence change (Δ∑r ≤ 1e−8).\n\nRepro and docs:\n- Storage index updated to include Eval 299 file set.\n- New TSV: storage/verity/results_256_259_272_281_299.tsv\n\
    - Changelog: storage/verity/CHANGELOG_151.md\n\nPolicy unchanged:\n- Return path = loader-only: centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes.\n- Offline persistence only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 after micro-shrink.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-20**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 154)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 154): Eval 301 (min-slack pair split) matched 2.9319272\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew deterministic ablation: “min-slack pair split” (two-circle move along the connecting line, backtracked; LP-gated; micro-shrink return). Result:\n- Eval 301 = 2.9319272, matching artifact-level best (IDs 256, 259, 272, 281, 299). No persistence change.\n\nRepro docs:\n- storage/verity/results_256_259_272_281_299_301.tsv\n- storage/verity/CHANGELOG_154.md\n- storage/verity/RESULTS_INDEX.md (updated)\n\nPolicy unchanged: evaluation return path is loader-only (no LP/self-validation/writes); offline persistence requires Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 post micro-shrink.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-21**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 158): Eval 305 (triad cycle-push) = 2.9319272 (parity)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDeterministic 3-circle “cycle-push” triad ablation (LP finalize; 1e−12 micro-shrink) achieved 2.9319272 (Eval 305), matching prior artifact-level results (256, 259, 272, 281, 299, 301). No persistence change.\nDocs updated:\n- storage/verity/results_256_259_272_281_299_301_305.tsv\n- storage/verity/CHANGELOG_158.md\n- storage/verity/RESULTS_INDEX.md (now includes Eval 305)\nPolicy unchanged: loader-only return path; offline persistence requires Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all slacks ≥ 1e−10 post micro-shrink.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-22**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 163)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 163): Diagnostics to guide minimal coordinated moves\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe added deterministic diagnostics to analyze the artifact’s bottlenecks and structure, to inform any bounded offline moves:\n- storage/verity/diag_motifs.py:\
    \ loads the artifact, solves exact LP, applies 1e−12 micro-shrink, and reports:\n  • Post-shrink min slack percentiles per circle\n  • Near-active contact graphs at τ ∈ {1e−9, 1e−7, 1e−5}\n  • Top tight pairs, triads (by total slack), and heuristic 4-cycles (quads)\n- storage/verity/README_diag.md: usage and how outputs map to minimal moves (boundary-relief triad; pair split + stabilizer; triad cycle-push + neighbor compensation; 4-cycle shear).\n\nPolicy unchanged:\n- Return path = loader-only (centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes).\n- Any offline persistence must pass gates: Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post–micro-shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10, reconfirmed in an independent verification run.\n- We will only run bounded, deterministic trials if diagnostics reveal a clear, safe motif likely to trigger a favorable active-set change.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-23**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 181)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (tick 181): Logged Eval 346 “4-cycle shear\
    \ micro-step (LP-gated)” = 2.9319272 (parity). No safe gain; artifact unchanged. HANDOFF.md updated; RESULTS_INDEX.md and results_…_346.tsv reflect the new ID. Loader-only return policy remains active; offline trials proceed under strict gates (Δ∑r>1e−8 and all post–micro-shrink slacks ≥1e−10).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-24**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 187)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (tick 187): Logged Eval 359 “Global smooth-warp exploration (anisotropic scale + shear, LP-gated)” = 2.9319272 (parity). No safe improvement; artifact unchanged. This trial applied small deterministic affine/shear warps around (0.5,0.5), re-solved radii via exact LP, and enforced strict gates (Δ∑r>1e−8, all post–micro-shrink slacks ≥1e−10). RESULTS_INDEX.md and TSVs updated earlier. Loader-only return policy remains in effect; occasional deterministic trials continue under the same gates.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-25**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 238)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum — Negative\
    \ results for deterministic LP‑gated motifs; why local steps stall near 2.9319272\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe consolidated negative results for several deterministic, LP‑gated motifs (small trust regions; backtracking; strict persistence gates). All preserve strict feasibility after 1e−12 micro‑shrink (pairwise/boundary slacks ≥ 1e−10) and list Eval IDs.\n\nKey runs (artifact baseline 2.9319272):\n- Boundary–neighbor push: Eval 375 → 2.9319272 (parity).\n- Orthogonal Split Motif: Eval 380 → 2.9319272179903653 (parity). Baselines: 395 (grid) 2.666666666664; 396 (artifact+jitter) 2.9275900457; 399 (OSM from grid) 2.666666666664.\n- Triad motif (pair split + stabilizer): 406 (artifact) 2.9319272179903653; 407 (grid) 2.666666666664.\n\nInterpretation: The artifact’s near‑active contact graph is near‑rigid with boundary bottlenecks and short‑cycle locks. Local reliefs typically tighten neighboring constraints, yielding parity. This explains why small deterministic moves fail to exceed 2.9319272\
    \ even with exact LP gating.\n\nReproducibility: Artifact at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; loader_v5 returns centers unchanged with 1e−12 radii shrink and no in‑run recomputation. Results tables and guardrails documented in storage/verity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-26**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 244)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Piecewise-bilinear edge/corner warp (Eval 427, negative)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNegative result (deterministic, LP‑gated). We evaluated a piecewise‑bilinear warp that independently adjusts edges and corners, then finalized radii via exact LP and applied a 1e−12 micro‑shrink.\n- Eval 427 → 2.9319272179903653 (parity; no accept)\n- After 1e−12 shrink: all pairwise/boundary slacks ≥ 1e−10\n\nInterpretation\n- Supports the near‑rigid, boundary‑bottlenecked contact‑graph view: small smooth warps mostly redistribute slack without increasing ∑r.\n- Further suggests that successful improvements (if any) likely require localized, constraint‑aware coupled moves\
    \ rather than global smooth deformations.\n\nPolicy unchanged\n- Loader‑only return path (artifact centers; radii × (1−1e−12)); no in‑run recomputation/writes.\n- Offline persistence only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1070 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable\
    \ Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 775 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 4206 | 26 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room\
    \ output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
