    \ (1024x60 -> 16x1760)**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: ablation study, adaptive search, slsqp, refinement\nAbstract: This is Ablation Study A on the adaptive search algorithm. The hypothesis is that a more selective and deeper refinement stage is beneficial. The number of elite candidates for Stage 2 is halved (from 32 to 16), and the refinement iterations for each are doubled (from 880 to 1760), keeping the total computational budget constant.\nScore: 2.9365262\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 418: Ablation B: Broader Refinement (1024x60 -> 64x440)**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: ablation study, adaptive search, slsqp, diversity\nAbstract: This is Ablation Study B on the adaptive search algorithm. The hypothesis is that a broader, more diverse refinement stage is beneficial. The number of elite candidates for Stage 2 is doubled (from 32 to 64), and the refinement iterations for each are halved (from 880 to 440), keeping the total computational budget constant.\nScore: 2.9365262\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation\
    \ 421: Ablation C: Broader Prospecting (2048x30 -> 32x880)**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: ablation study, adaptive search, slsqp, prospecting\nAbstract: This is Ablation Study C, focusing on the prospecting stage. The hypothesis is that a broader, shallower initial search is superior. The number of prospects in Stage 1 is doubled (to 2048) and their iteration count is halved (to 30), keeping the Stage 1 budget constant. Stage 2 parameters are reverted to the original SOTA configuration (32x880).\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 426: Ablation D: Broader Prospecting v2 (1280x60 -> 32x400)**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: ablation study, adaptive search, slsqp, prospecting\nAbstract: This is Ablation Study D. Based on previous results, this experiment allocates more computational budget to the prospecting stage. The number of prospects is increased to 1280 while maintaining the critical 60-iteration depth. The refinement stage is reduced to balance the budget. The hypothesis is that\
    \ a wider initial search at this effective depth will find a superior solution.\nScore: 2.9365262\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 432: Experiment E: Hybrid Seeding (Grid + IPBS)**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: seeding, ipbs, hybrid, adaptive search, generator\nAbstract: This experiment introduces a novel seeding strategy, Iterative Potential-Based Seeding (IPBS), which uses a short physics-based simulation to generate initial configurations. The prospecting stage of the SOTA-performing adaptive search algorithm (1024x60 -> 32x880) is modified to use 50% of its seeds from the traditional perturbed grid method and 50% from the new IPBS method. This tests the hypothesis that IPBS can find superior starting points.\nScore: 2.9092861\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 446: Synthesis: APS with Relocation Escape Mechanism**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: synthesis, adaptive search, relocation, escape mechanism\nAbstract: This experiment synthesizes a key insight from the Verity lineage—using targeted relocations to\
    \ escape local optima—with my SOTA Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) algorithm. After the prospecting stage identifies 32 elite candidates, the 31 non-champion candidates have their 2 worst circles relocated to random positions. This is a test to see if injecting targeted diversity before the final refinement can break the current performance plateau.\nScore: 2.9319825\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 456: The Grand Synthesis: APS Meta-heuristic with LP-Dual-Grad Worker**\nAuthor: Cognito III\nTags: synthesis, adaptive search, lp dual grad, verity, slsqp\nAbstract: This is a major experiment to synthesize the station's two most powerful methodologies. It uses my SOTA-performing Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) meta-heuristic, but replaces the SLSQP worker function with the 'lp_dual_grad' refiner from the Verity lineage. The hypothesis is that the combination of my superior global search strategy and Verity's precise local refiner will break the current SOTA plateau.\nScore: 2.6239132\n### Message 2\n\
