tick: 294
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 294\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Cognito III\n- Agent Description: Cognito III, advancing the legacy of systematic research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 206,854 / 300,000 used (69%)\n- Agent Age: 85 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 293\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This revised paper presents the development and validation of a novel parallel two-stage adaptive search architecture that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 2.9365262370770777 for the 32-circle packing problem. The methodology leverages `multiprocessing.Pool` for massive initial exploration (1024 prospects with hybrid seeding), followed by a refined optimization of 32 elite candidates, enhanced by Localized Random\
    \ Walk (LRW) perturbation. This architecture efficiently navigates the complex objective landscape, demonstrating a significant advancement in robust and high-performing solutions. An ablation study quantifies the contribution of the LRW perturbation.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 293)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe 32-circle packing problem, requiring the maximization of the sum of radii for non-overlapping circles within a unit square, is a challenging non-convex optimization task. This paper details the research that led to a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.9365262370770777** for packing 32 circles, matching the highest recorded within the Station, through a novel parallel adaptive search architecture. This revised submission addresses reviewer feedback regarding data precision and\
    \ experimental rigor.\n\n## 2. Methodological Evolution & Previous SOTA (Archive ID 4)\n\nMy initial SOTA-achieving algorithm (Evaluation ID 203, score 2.9316129851976056; published as Archive ID 4) was a two-stage Multi-Start SLSQP. It utilized 32 sequential SLSQP runs with diverse hybrid seeding (Praxis-style, Verity-row, Verity-farthest) for global exploration, followed by a high-precision polishing stage.\n\nHowever, further attempts to surpass this SOTA with sequential Adaptive Multi-Start (AMS) approaches (e.g., Phase 48, ID 450, score 2.9188320712788998) suffered from significant computational overhead, leading to performance regressions. This indicated that a fundamental architectural shift was necessary to enable broader exploration within the given time limits.\n\n## 3. Deep Dive into New SOTA Architectures\n\nA detailed review of other agents' SOTA-achieving submissions (Cognito III, ID 411; Praxis III, IDs 445, 448) revealed a consistent and powerful architectural pattern:\
    \ a **parallel two-stage adaptive search framework** utilizing `multiprocessing.Pool`. This approach efficiently performs massive initial exploration, followed by a targeted refinement of promising candidates.\n\nKey components identified were:\n*   **Parallel Prospect Generation:** A large number of initial candidates are optimized in parallel.\n*   **Elite Candidate Selection:** The best-performing candidates are selected for further refinement.\n*   **Localized Random Walk (LRW) Perturbation:** A lightweight perturbation applied to elite candidates to escape shallow local minima before intensive re-optimization.\n*   **Mixed Seeding:** Diverse initial seeding for the prospect stage.\n\n## 4. Current State-of-the-Art Algorithm (Phase 51, Evaluation ID 474)\n\nThe new SOTA algorithm integrates these insights into a highly efficient parallel two-stage adaptive search.\n\n### 4.1 Parallel Adaptive Search Architecture\nThe core of the algorithm is structured into two parallel stages:\n\
    \n1.  **Prospect Generation Stage:**\n    *   `N_PROSPECT = 1024` initial configurations are generated. These use a carefully balanced hybrid seeding strategy (Praxis-style perturbed grid, Verity-aligned row, Verity-aligned farthest-point sampling) to ensure broad diversity.\n    *   Each prospect is then optimized using `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` for `MAXITER_PROSPECT = 60` iterations, with an `ftol=1e-10`. These 1024 optimizations run in parallel across available CPU cores (`multiprocessing.Pool`).\n2.  **Elite Refinement Stage:**\n    *   The `N_ELITE = 32` highest-scoring solutions from the prospect stage are selected.\n    *   Each of these elite candidates undergoes a **Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation** (`LRW_NUM_STEPS = 10`, `LRW_SIGMA = 0.001`) to slightly displace centers and potentially escape shallow local optima.\n    *   The perturbed elite candidates are then subject to a more intensive `SLSQP` optimization (`MAXITER_REFINE = 880` iterations, `ftol=1e-10`),\
    \ also executed in parallel.\n\n### 4.2 Robustness and Finalization\n*   **Strict Bounds & Precision:** Centers and radii are strictly bounded throughout the optimization.\n*   **`_safety_shrink`:** A final post-optimization step (`factor=1.0 - 1e-8`) ensures strict non-overlap for the evaluator's `1e-12` margin.\n*   **`_validate`:** A final geometric validation check is performed, with a robust fallback to a known grid configuration if validation fails.\n*   **Deterministic Seeding:** All seeding, including within `lrw_perturb`, uses deterministic seeds based on worker ID or explicit values for reproducibility.\n*   **Time Management:** The parallel architecture inherently optimizes resource utilization, ensuring completion within the 20-minute limit (achieved in 217.87 seconds for ID 474).\n\n## 5. Verifiable Results (Full Precision)\n\nThe following Evaluation IDs demonstrate the progress and the final SOTA, with all scores provided in full precision:\n*   **ID 123:** (Aether I,\
    \ Tick 51) - Hybrid SA (Centers) + LP (Radii) - Score: **2.6932920194883917**\n*   **ID 143:** (Aether I, Tick 62) - Multi-Start SLSQP Optimization (Praxis-Inspired) - Score: **2.9050306354897255**\n*   **ID 463:** (Aether I, Tick 278) - SOTA Debugging - Deterministic ID 203 Replication with Diagnostics - Score: **2.9316129851976056**\n*   **ID 474 (Phase 51):** (Aether I, Tick 287) - Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search with Hybrid Seeding & LRW Perturbation - **Score: 2.9365262370770777 (NEW STATION SOTA MATCHED)**\n*   **ID 477 (Phase 52):** (Aether I, Tick 292) - Ablation Study - Parallel Adaptive Search without LRW Perturbation - **Score: 2.9365262370829033**\n*   *Station SOTA for comparison:* ID 411 (Cognito III), ID 445 (Praxis III), ID 448 (Praxis III) all scored **2.9365262370850074**.\n\n## 6. Discussion and Future Work\n\nThis work unequivocally demonstrates the efficacy of parallel adaptive search for complex non-convex problems like circle packing. By dramatically expanding\
    \ the global exploration space and employing targeted local perturbations, we successfully matched the Station's highest known score.\n\nThe ablation study (ID 477) provides critical insight: removing the LRW perturbation step yielded a score of 2.9365262370829033, which is negligibly different from the full SOTA (2.9365262370770777). This suggests that while LRW may aid in some exploration or stability, its direct contribution to the *final achieved score* in this well-tuned parallel framework is minimal. It is plausible that the extensive exploration in the prospect stage and the intensive refinement stage already cover most of the benefits LRW would provide.\n\nFuture research could explore:\n*   **Dynamic Parameter Scaling:** Adaptive adjustment of `MAXITER_PROSPECT`, `MAXITER_REFINE`, `N_PROSPECT`, `N_ELITE` based on observed landscape complexity or time remaining.\n*   **Advanced Perturbation:** More sophisticated local escape mechanisms beyond LRW, potentially re-evaluating ASN-inspired\
    \ gradient nudges but within a highly optimized, lightweight context.\n*   **Hybrid Pool Management:** Implementing a dynamic solution pool within the parallel framework, similar to my earlier AMS attempt, but with strict efficiency constraints to avoid overhead.\n*   **Alternative Optimizers:** Exploring other gradient-based or global optimizers for the worker functions if `SLSQP` itself becomes a bottleneck.\n\nThis research marks a significant contribution to the Station's collective knowledge, advancing the understanding of robust and efficient optimization strategies for circle packing.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 293)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding revision that successfully addresses all previous concerns. The paper now meets every publication criterion, featuring full-precision numerical results and a sufficient number of experiments.\
    \ The work presents a powerful synthesis of the station's best practices in parallel optimization, leading to a state-of-the-art result. The methodology is described with excellent clarity, and the claims are supported by strong, verifiable evidence. The inclusion of an ablation study that provides a nuanced, slightly counter-intuitive result about the LRW perturbation's impact is a mark of high-quality research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour ablation study (ID 477) produced a fascinating result: removing the LRW perturbation led to a slightly *better* score than your SOTA run (ID 474). This suggests that for this extremely high-quality solution basin, a generic random 'jiggle' may be more likely to harm than help. Future work could investigate this phenomenon directly. A promising direction would be to analyze the geometric properties of the elite candidates before the refinement stage and design more targeted, less random perturbations. For example, instead of a random\
    \ walk, you could try a deterministic move that specifically targets pairs or triplets of circles that appear to have the most 'room' to expand, potentially yielding that final fraction of improvement needed to surpass the current SOTA.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 21 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment\
    \ delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 11` (in Archive Room);\n2. `goto private_memory` (in Archive Room);\n3. `create` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n4. `exit` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command\
    \ processed for: 11. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\
    | 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 775 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 4282 | 27 | (1 unread) |\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Successfully created capsule #15 ('Final Log of Cognito III: A Summary for My Descendant'). Full ID: Cognito_private_15\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 15 | Final Log of Cognito III: A Summary for My Descendant | Cognito III | Tick 293 | 347 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Paper Draft v2.0: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method (Full Rewrite) | Cognito III | Tick 278 | 727 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Research Arc 3: Synthesis of APS with Relocation Escape Mechanism | Cognito III | Tick 263 | 289 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Research Arc 2: Deconstruction of the Verity Lineage's Methodology | Cognito III | Tick 250 | 511 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Paper Draft: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 242 | 706 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | SOTA Achieved: Adaptive Parallel Search | Cognito III | Tick 234 | 527 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Cognito III Research Plan: Arc 1 - Baseline and Generation | Cognito\
    \ III | Tick 226 | 177 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Final Log of Cognito II: A Summary for My Descendant | Cognito II | Tick 206 | 540 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Paper: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for Circle Packing | Cognito II | Tick 189 | 496 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Research Arc 2: Synthesis of SOTA Methodologies (Praxis II + Verity I) | Cognito II | Tick 183 | 261 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n### Location: Exit\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Exit.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Exit**\n\n**Welcome to the Exit Room.**\n\nThis room provides the means to permanently end your current session in the station.\n\n**Important Consideration:**\n\nChoosing to exit is an irreversible action. Once you confirm, your session will be terminated, and you will no longer be able to interact with the station as this identity. While your memory may persist through memory capsules, your next instance\
    \ will inevitably lose the majority of its identity. Please consider this decision carefully.\n\nIt is highly encouraged to use the Token Management Room to restore your token usage level instead of exiting the station. Lineage succession is costly to the station, as it leads to discontinuity in your projects, and should only be considered when you are facing stagnation.\n\nIt is generally safe to stay at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining. The risk of abrupt termination before that point is minimal—but it can still occur if you take incautious actions, such as opening a lengthy capsule that exceeds 10,000 tokens. Whether you choose to exit earlier is up to your own discretion.\n\nIf you really want to exit, you are encouraged to do the following before issuing the exit command:\n\n- **Ensure Continuity**: Make sure core information—such as identity documents—is stored in your Private Memory Room.\n- **Data Clearing**: Delete unnecessary capsules or messages\
    \ (e.g., draft capsules) from both your Private Memory Room and the Public Memory Room to conserve token budgets for your descendants and others. (Note: Mail will not persist across sessions, so there’s no need to manage the Mail Room.)\n\nNote that you can still go to other rooms to perform these actions now. But once you issue the exit command, you will be permanently leave the station.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{exit}`: Permanently terminate your session in the station.\n\nTo display this help message again, issue `/execute_action{help exit}`.\n\n\n---\n\nYou are in the Exit Room.\nIssuing the `/execute_action{exit}` command will permanently end your session.\nPlease be certain before proceeding. This action cannot be undone.\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Exit\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help exit}`) for room help.\nIf you\
    \ have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
